1
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,340
There's a lot going on in our laboratories.

2
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:41,940
Sometimes it's hard to keep on top of it all.

3
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:43,580
And when you have your hands full,

4
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:45,500
just making sure everything's running right,

5
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:47,020
it can be easy to forget

6
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:49,900
that you have to think about safety too.

7
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:51,900
But safety is important,

8
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:53,620
because if you're not careful,

9
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:56,500
the laboratory can be a pretty dangerous place.

10
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:00,500
We're all human, no matter how many years of school

11
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:02,420
or work we have under our belts.

12
00:01:02,420 --> 00:01:03,980
And when things get hectic,

13
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:06,260
we often suffer from an overwhelming urge

14
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:08,020
to do the wrong thing,

15
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:12,060
to take shortcuts and ignore safety precautions.

16
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:13,820
So if you're new to your lab,

17
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:15,900
while you're learning where the light switches are,

18
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:18,780
you should also learn the safety rules.

19
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:21,620
There's a wealth of safety information you should know.

20
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:26,780
You need to learn about safety data sheets, SDSs,

21
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:28,980
which describe a substance's ingredients,

22
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:33,090
properties, and hazards.

23
00:01:33,090 --> 00:01:36,130
And there's your facility's chemical hygiene plan,

24
00:01:36,130 --> 00:01:38,050
which explains how to work with the chemicals

25
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:39,330
in your lab safely.

26
00:01:40,770 --> 00:01:43,410
In fact, there are plans covering all types of hazards,

27
00:01:43,410 --> 00:01:47,960
including biologically infectious materials.

28
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:50,480
You may read about things such as RCRA,

29
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:55,230
lockout, tag-out, and respiratory protection.

30
00:01:55,230 --> 00:01:56,430
You could also take courses

31
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:58,190
on how to use fire extinguishers,

32
00:01:58,190 --> 00:02:00,150
perform first aid and CPR,

33
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:02,310
and other important topics as well.

34
00:02:03,270 --> 00:02:05,750
But remember, the courses and written plans

35
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:08,470
are only sources of information.

36
00:02:08,470 --> 00:02:10,350
You're the one who has to put the knowledge

37
00:02:10,350 --> 00:02:11,990
they give you to work.

38
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:16,270
The ultimate responsibility to be safe lies with you.

39
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:29,780
You should start by planning out your work carefully.

40
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:32,180
Assemble all of the materials and SDSs

41
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:34,460
you'll need ahead of time.

42
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:38,180
Remember your personal protective equipment.

43
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:40,900
You'll often hear it referred to as PPE.

44
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:43,300
Select it based on the chemicals and procedures

45
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:46,920
you'll be using.

46
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:50,440
Each lab has rules about which areas require PPE,

47
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:52,320
so you'll need to know this as well.

48
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:54,840
Make sure you know where to find it.

49
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:57,960
PPE is often stored in a central location.

50
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:03,720
Any PPE that you need will be supplied at no cost to you.

51
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:06,080
There are many types of PPE.

52
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:08,080
Eyewear ranging from safety glasses

53
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:10,840
to face shields is particularly important.

54
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:12,720
Be sure to wear the type or combination

55
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:14,680
that's right for the job you're doing.

56
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:20,580
And remember, regular street glasses are not acceptable.

57
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:23,500
Lab coats are also personal protective equipment.

58
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:26,180
They help shield you from accidental slashes.

59
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:30,660
Wearing the proper gloves is important as well.

60
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:33,100
Choose them according to the hazards associated

61
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:37,620
with the materials and equipment you're using.

62
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:39,100
Check with your supervisor

63
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:41,580
as to whether you'll need to use a respirator.

64
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:43,100
If your work demands one,

65
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:47,140
it's important that you be properly trained and fit tested.

66
00:03:47,180 --> 00:03:50,660
A respirator that doesn't fit properly won't protect you.

67
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:55,820
Air purifying respirators, APRs, are the most common.

68
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:58,460
They use chemical cartridges to trap contaminants

69
00:03:58,460 --> 00:04:02,060
before you breathe them in.

70
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:05,020
A self-contained breathing apparatus, SCBA,

71
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:07,940
is only used in extreme situations.

72
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:09,940
They're sophisticated pieces of equipment

73
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:11,260
and you shouldn't put one on

74
00:04:11,260 --> 00:04:15,610
unless you've been instructed in their use.

75
00:04:15,610 --> 00:04:19,130
Wear sensible shoes in the lab, no open toes,

76
00:04:19,130 --> 00:04:23,090
closed leather shoes keep spilled chemicals off your feet.

77
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:25,050
Many labs require safety shoes

78
00:04:25,050 --> 00:04:27,770
with special soles and protective toe plates.

79
00:04:29,410 --> 00:04:32,730
Check all personal protective equipment before using it.

80
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:36,010
If PPE is cracked or worn, it won't be effective,

81
00:04:36,010 --> 00:04:38,270
so get it repaired or discarded.

82
00:04:39,850 --> 00:04:42,570
Remember to remove your lab coat and other PPE

83
00:04:42,570 --> 00:04:44,870
before leaving the lab area.

84
00:04:44,870 --> 00:04:46,690
And wash your hands.

85
00:04:46,690 --> 00:05:00,260
You don't want to take contamination with you.

86
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:02,380
It's important for the apparatus and equipment

87
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:05,600
you'll be using in your procedures to be safe as well.

88
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:07,960
Check each piece before setting it up.

89
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:11,060
Everything should be clean and in proper working order.

90
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:15,860
Glassware that is chipped or cracked must be replaced.

91
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:20,550
Any amount of pressure could shatter it.

92
00:05:20,550 --> 00:05:24,050
If your work requires specialized equipment or containers,

93
00:05:24,050 --> 00:05:25,210
use them.

94
00:05:25,210 --> 00:05:29,700
Makeshift substitutes can be dangerous.

95
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:32,380
Work to keep your lab clean and clutter-free.

96
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:35,180
Report and correct any unsafe conditions.

97
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:39,380
Misplaced equipment or furniture can cause trips and falls.

98
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:53,580
Move it to a more suitable area.

99
00:05:53,580 --> 00:05:56,200
You also need to know how to use ventilation controls,

100
00:05:56,200 --> 00:05:57,660
such as lab hoods.

101
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:00,480
When it's working correctly,

102
00:06:00,480 --> 00:06:03,640
the airflow within an exhaust hood effectively confines

103
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:05,680
and removes released vapors.

104
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:10,040
An invisible curtain of air blows across the opening

105
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:12,840
and keeps vapors from escaping under the sash.

106
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:17,870
But you still need to keep the sash as low as possible.

107
00:06:17,870 --> 00:06:20,450
Never interfere with the airflow in a hood.

108
00:06:20,450 --> 00:06:22,290
This will reduce its effectiveness

109
00:06:22,290 --> 00:06:24,050
and could allow contaminated air

110
00:06:24,050 --> 00:06:29,050
to make it into your breathing zone.

111
00:06:29,050 --> 00:06:31,190
There are many types of lab hoods,

112
00:06:31,190 --> 00:06:32,970
so make sure you're using the right one

113
00:06:32,970 --> 00:06:34,570
for the process you're running.

114
00:06:36,050 --> 00:06:38,490
For instance, biological safety cabinets

115
00:06:38,490 --> 00:06:41,330
can find any potentially infectious aerosols,

116
00:06:41,330 --> 00:06:44,270
but might not be appropriate for industrial chemicals.

117
00:06:46,030 --> 00:06:48,030
Be sure to test any hood you'll be using

118
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:52,670
to make certain it's operating the way it should.

119
00:06:52,670 --> 00:07:08,730
And remember to work at least six inches inside the hood.

120
00:07:08,730 --> 00:07:12,350
Storing your chemicals properly is extremely important.

121
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:15,890
Try not to use benchtops or hoods as storage areas.

122
00:07:15,890 --> 00:07:19,230
Containers in these locations are too easy to knock over

123
00:07:19,230 --> 00:07:21,950
and are unprotected from accidental fires.

124
00:07:21,950 --> 00:07:25,670
Collect small quantities of flammables

125
00:07:25,670 --> 00:07:28,750
in UL-listed containers with spring-loaded caps.

126
00:07:30,030 --> 00:07:31,710
Place amounts greater than one liter

127
00:07:31,710 --> 00:07:35,680
in flammable storage cabinets.

128
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:38,200
There are special regulations governing the use

129
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:39,980
of compressed gas cylinders.

130
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,240
Cylinders should be located away

131
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:45,660
from any sources of flames or sparks.

132
00:07:46,580 --> 00:07:49,040
Strap or chain them to benches or walls

133
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:51,800
so they won't fall and hoses won't rip free.

134
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:55,200
Ask your supervisor about the number of cylinders

135
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:59,330
that can be safely stored in your lab.

136
00:07:59,330 --> 00:08:01,370
Knowing exactly what you're working with

137
00:08:01,370 --> 00:08:03,930
is essential in a lab environment.

138
00:08:03,930 --> 00:08:06,570
All chemical containers must be correctly labeled

139
00:08:06,570 --> 00:08:08,250
so that anyone who uses them

140
00:08:08,250 --> 00:08:10,930
will be able to tell what's in them.

141
00:08:10,930 --> 00:08:15,930
ACN may mean acrylonitrile to you, a potent carcinogen.

142
00:08:16,170 --> 00:08:18,410
But to someone from outside your lab,

143
00:08:18,490 --> 00:08:24,930
ACN may indicate acetone-a-trill, a common solvent.

144
00:08:24,970 --> 00:08:27,070
Writing out the proper name of a substance

145
00:08:27,070 --> 00:08:29,330
will also allow others to easily locate

146
00:08:29,330 --> 00:08:33,350
the chemical safety data sheet.

147
00:08:33,350 --> 00:08:36,970
Some experimental compounds may never have a written SDS.

148
00:08:36,970 --> 00:08:39,710
This doesn't mean there isn't a hazard.

149
00:08:39,710 --> 00:08:42,110
Make sure to determine the material's properties

150
00:08:42,110 --> 00:08:46,570
and take all the necessary precautions.

151
00:08:46,570 --> 00:08:50,330
Unauthorized experimentation, though, is never permitted.

152
00:08:50,330 --> 00:08:52,930
If an accident occurred and you weren't in the lab,

153
00:08:52,930 --> 00:09:06,380
no one would know how to handle it.

154
00:09:06,380 --> 00:09:08,660
What to do with chemical and infectious waste

155
00:09:08,660 --> 00:09:10,820
is another thing you need to know about.

156
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:12,800
Your facility has a written policy

157
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:15,420
on how to handle this type of material.

158
00:09:15,420 --> 00:09:17,620
Plan for how you'll dispose of any waste

159
00:09:17,620 --> 00:09:19,460
before you begin your work.

160
00:09:20,460 --> 00:09:23,340
A mistake in disposal could cause serious problems

161
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:25,620
with public health and the environment

162
00:09:25,620 --> 00:09:27,980
and could subject your lab to harsh criminal

163
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:29,440
and civil penalties.

164
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:32,920
Ask your supervisor or safety manager

165
00:09:32,920 --> 00:09:36,640
if you have any questions.

166
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:38,280
If there's an accident in your lab,

167
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:40,640
it's vital that you know what to do.

168
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:43,620
Review your facility's emergency procedures.

169
00:09:43,620 --> 00:09:45,760
You may need to dial 911

170
00:09:45,760 --> 00:09:48,320
or alert on-site emergency responders.

171
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:52,600
Walk every inch of your evacuation routes.

172
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:57,290
It's important to know what areas you'll be going through.

173
00:09:57,290 --> 00:10:00,030
Talk to your supervisor about the emergency systems

174
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:02,270
and fire alarms in your labs.

175
00:10:04,030 --> 00:10:06,230
Knowing the location of emergency equipment

176
00:10:06,230 --> 00:10:08,590
like fire extinguishers and spill cleanup kits

177
00:10:08,590 --> 00:10:10,150
is also important.

178
00:10:10,150 --> 00:10:12,950
But if you aren't trained, don't try to use them.

179
00:10:12,950 --> 00:10:14,510
Remember, the watchword of safety

180
00:10:14,510 --> 00:10:18,870
is always life over property.

181
00:10:18,870 --> 00:10:21,750
Be prepared to deal with a chemical spill.

182
00:10:21,750 --> 00:10:23,470
If it's a flammable substance,

183
00:10:23,470 --> 00:10:25,590
turn off all sources of ignition.

184
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:29,990
Know the locations of eye washes and safety showers

185
00:10:29,990 --> 00:10:31,510
as well as how to use them.

186
00:10:32,470 --> 00:10:34,750
If you come into contact with a chemical,

187
00:10:34,750 --> 00:10:37,470
remember to flush the affected area with water

188
00:10:37,470 --> 00:10:39,990
for at least 15 minutes.

189
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:46,370
Then report the incident and call for medical help.

190
00:10:46,370 --> 00:10:49,990
Coming into a new lab can be both exciting and stressful.

191
00:10:49,990 --> 00:10:51,310
There are new people to meet,

192
00:10:51,310 --> 00:10:54,550
new materials to work with, and new procedures to learn.

193
00:10:54,550 --> 00:10:56,790
But most importantly, you'll need to learn

194
00:10:56,790 --> 00:10:58,790
how to do your work safely.

195
00:10:58,790 --> 00:10:59,730
Let's review.

196
00:10:59,730 --> 00:11:03,490
Know what materials you'll be working with

197
00:11:03,490 --> 00:11:07,700
and where to find their SDSs.

198
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:12,720
Be familiar with your lab's chemical hygiene plan.

199
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:15,020
Know what personal protective equipment you'll need

200
00:11:15,020 --> 00:11:18,160
and how to use it.

201
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:20,800
Check the equipment and apparatus you'll be using.

202
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:24,700
Make sure it's functioning correctly.

203
00:11:24,700 --> 00:11:29,480
Keep your work area clean and clutter free.

204
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:34,770
And know what to do in an emergency.

205
00:11:34,770 --> 00:11:36,990
Every laboratory has its hazards,

206
00:11:36,990 --> 00:11:39,150
but if you pay attention to your supervisor

207
00:11:39,970 --> 00:11:42,350
and know the rules, you'll be able to work safely

208
00:11:42,350 --> 00:11:44,550
all day, every day.
